    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Narcolepsy without cataplexy due to a medical condition, provisional (G47.429)</content>. All four captured spells began at sleep onset with no ictal pattern, so they are sleep attacks, not seizures. Polysomnography showed REM sleep within 15 minutes of sleep onset and an apnea-hypopnea index of 4, excluding sleep apnea. The multiple sleep latency test showed a mean sleep latency of 4.5 minutes with two sleep-onset REM periods in five naps. With the overnight sleep-onset REM period this meets narcolepsy criteria; the diagnosis stays provisional because it follows a brain injury. PROMIS sleep-related impairment T-score 67. [S]<list><item>Start modafinil 100 mg each morning.</item><item>Two scheduled 15-minute naps a day.</item><item>Recheck blood pressure, heart rate, and PROMIS score at the three-month review.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic post-traumatic headache, partial response (G44.329)</content>. Fewer headache days on candesartan 8 mg. Potassium 4.6 and creatinine 0.90 (eGFR 67) are stable; blood pressure 132/80 mmHg. [S]<list><item>Increase candesartan to 16 mg daily.</item><item>Judge the preventive effect at the three-month review.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Medication-overuse headache (G44.40)</content>. No ibuprofen for six weeks and acetaminophen about once a week; goal met. [S]<list><item>Keep acute medicine to no more than 2 days a week.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Ambulatory EEG with video</td><td>When routine EEG is not diagnostic</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Reviewed today: four typical spells captured, each at sleep onset with no ictal pattern</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Overnight polysomnography and multiple sleep latency test</td><td>For excessive daytime sleepiness</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Reviewed today: apnea-hypopnea index 4; mean sleep latency 4.5 minutes with two sleep-onset REM periods</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Antiseizure medicine</td><td>Only if spells prove epileptic</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Spells are sleep attacks; no antiseizure medicine</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PROMIS sleep-related impairment</td><td>At baseline and after treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>T-score 67, little changed from 68 before treatment</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Modafinil: blood pressure and heart rate</td><td>Before starting and at follow-up</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Baseline 132/80 mmHg, pulse 72; started today at 100 mg for an older adult</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Basic metabolic panel (potassium and creatinine) on candesartan</td><td>Before starting and a few weeks after; then periodically</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Four weeks after starting: potassium 4.6, creatinine 0.90, eGFR 67, sodium and glucose normal</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Blood pressure, heart rate, and weight</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>132/80 mmHg, pulse 72, weight 51.5 kg</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Headache diary (headache days and acute-medicine days)</td><td>Daily</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Reviewed; fewer headache days since starting candesartan</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Acute headache medicine on no more than 2 days a week</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Acetaminophen about once a week; no ibuprofen</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Headache days per three months</td><td>2 to 3 months after starting prevention</td><td>Due</td><td>At the three-month review</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Safety advice for sudden sleep episodes</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Reviewed; scheduled naps added</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression screen (PHQ-9)</td><td>At baseline and at follow-up</td><td>Due</td><td>At the three-month review</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Stop daily over-the-counter analgesics and limit acute headache medicine to no more than 2 days a week. Outcome by 2026-05-18: No ibuprofen since the first visit; acetaminophen about once a week [S]</item><item>Cut headache days by at least half by the three-month review. In progress; target 2026-08-17 [S]</item><item>Reduce daytime sleep attacks and lower the PROMIS sleep-related impairment T-score below 60. In progress; target 2026-08-17 [S]</item><item>Establish whether the spells are epileptic before considering antiseizure medicine. Outcome by 2026-05-18: Typical spells captured on ambulatory EEG were sleep onset; no antiseizure medicine needed [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Evidence-based guideline: Management of an unprovoked first seizure in adults (Krumholz A, et al. Neurology 2015;84(16):1705-1713) (2015); Treatment of central disorders of hypersomnolence: an American Academy of Sleep Medicine clinical practice guideline (Maski K, et al. J Clin Sleep Med 2021;17(9):1881-1893) (2021); The International Classification of Headache Disorders, 3rd edition (Cephalalgia 2018;38(1):1-211) (2018); The American Headache Society Consensus Statement: Update on integrating new migraine treatments into clinical practice (Ailani J, et al. Headache 2021;61(7):1021-1039) (2021)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Three-month sleepiness and headache review with potassium and creatinine, PROMIS score, headache-day count, and PHQ-9. Next scheduled visit 2026-08-17: three-month sleepiness and headache review. [S]</paragraph></text>

